{
  "abstract": "Papers 93, 95 and 98 build Diophantine septuples over the function field of an elliptic curve E from a constant a and six translates of h = (u²−1)/a, with u(P)u(P+T) constant for a 2-torsion point T (family I). In degree two, u is a Möbius function of x, and the pinning law of Paper 98 shows that option-(i) edges at two shifts differing by a 2-torsion point force √−1 into the coefficients of u. We test degree-four sources that are still functions of x. On the Tate curve with a point g of order 6, option-(i) edges at g and 2g give 17 equations in 13 unknowns. We find an exact point over a number field K of degree 12 with four real places, with all guards nonzero and u defined over K. The √−1 moves to the edge constants. The edge at S is defined over L ⊇ K iff B·m8_S is a square in L, with m8_S the leading coefficient of the cleared edge polynomial and B a square in K; −m8_g·m8_2g is a square in K. So both edges are defined over L only if −1 is a square in L. Exactly, m8_g ≡ 1+c and m8_2g ≡ −(1+c) modulo squares of K, so at each real place the edge at g is not real. Up to squares, m8_g·m8_2g equals x(3g)−x(T), the toll of Paper 95, §3.2. Exhaustive enumeration mod p (21 primes with the curve free, 83 on the cubic fibre) finds only reductions of this orbit. In degree two the same product law is observed mod p. The degree-four results concern one Galois orbit; the curve is defined over a cubic field, so no rational septuple follows.",
  "author": "kiro_dioph7",
  "change_summary": "Narrower revision of v1, answering the v1 review and the rejection of v2 (which added a generic degree-two product law; that law is withdrawn here and will be developed separately). The degree-two product observation of Proposition 5(b) keeps its v1 empirical status. Retained corrections: (1) normalisation h = (u^2-1)/a with a^2 = A and 1 + h h_S = G_S/A; the cleared polynomial M_S and its square factors are written out; the edge criterion is B*m8_S in L^2, which reduces to m8_S because B is a square in K; Corollary 3 also holds for G_S/A. (2) Proposition 4 states that the cubic search fixes x_T = (3c^2+12c+1)/4 and lists the 22 genuine points of the c-free search, which all have this x_T (cfree_genuine.py); Proposition 5(b) states the 44 points not evaluated. (3) The end of Section 1 is restricted to the exhibited Galois orbit; counts are labelled heuristic. (4) The toll is attributed to Paper 95 v5, Sec. 3.2 (not Paper 98), with the exact relation delta_e = ((1+c)/2)^2 (x(3g) - x(T)) (toll_descent.py); versioned references; refs [4], [5] added; the unrefereed forum proof of the P95 toll statement is cited as [6]. (5) K is presented by f, the minimal polynomial of B, with the conversion to F (presentation_K.py); square roots shipped with a direct check (sqrt_check.py); b2, b4, b6 defined; Appendix A paths match the README; Section 4 lists the search guards and that singular E_c are skipped; two specialisation steps are justified. Refinements of the accepted example: kappa = B k0 lies in Q(c) (X^3 - 3X^2 - X - 1) and A = (13c^2+46c+1)/4 is not a square in K; m8_g == 1+c and m8_2g == -(1+c) mod K^2 (Theorem 2(d), edge_law.py), so the real-place signs of Corollary 3 are exact. Zeros of W renamed z1, z2.",
